{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices across almost the entire 30-day horizon (often by ~2–8 EUR). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Downtrend underreaction: when the recent history shows a clear sell-off, the model projects a flatter/less negative trajectory than realized. Increase negative drift (steepen the decline) especially for days ~10–30.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: short horizon (days 1–5) errors are smaller; medium/long horizon (days ~15–30) errors widen materially. Use a horizon-increasing downward adjustment (e.g., larger haircut beyond 2 weeks).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model reverts upward/sideways too quickly after a drop, while the true series continues trending lower. Reduce upward mean reversion strength after bearish regimes; allow trend persistence.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot: model often lifts/holds prices in the back half (sometimes even rising), but outcomes keep falling. Cap late-horizon rebounds unless confirmed by recent positive momentum."
  ]
}
